<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">Part III &mdash; Seams and Return &middot; Wormholes and Inverted Tori</p>
  <h1>Wormholes and Inverted Tori</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">An inverted torus can describe a global return of orientation. A wormhole must do
    more: it must be a Lorentzian spacetime geometry with a throat, a stress&ndash;energy source, stable causal
    passage and an observable consequence. This page asks when the first picture could become the second.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The proposal in one sentence</p>
    <h2 id="t-proposal">A Throat Could Localise the Seam, but Topology Alone Cannot Hold It Open</h2>
    <p class="lede">TTI may use an orientation-reversing torus map to specify what changes between two
      mouths; it still owes the metric that joins them, the matter that supports the throat and the causal
      law that lets anything cross.</p>
    <p>The companion <a href="/inverted-toruses/">inverted-torus universe</a> is a global construction. Its
      toroidal slices return through a map \(f:T^2\to T^2\) with \(\det f_*=-1\). No local tunnel is
      required. A wormhole is different: two regions are connected by a spacetime throat, and Einstein&rsquo;s
      equations ask what stress&ndash;energy makes that geometry possible.</p>
    <p>This page therefore treats &ldquo;wormhole&rdquo; as a physical promotion with a higher burden of proof,
      not as a synonym for seam, portal, inversion or hole in a drawn doughnut.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted small">Grading: <span class="sci">&#9670; established mathematics or physics</span>
      &middot; <span class="spec">&#9670; a conditional TTI mechanism</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; philosophical image, stated as image</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Three objects, three burdens</p>
    <h2 id="t-three-objects">Do Not Call Every Return a Wormhole</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid three">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>A torus \(T^2\)</h3>
      <p>A two-dimensional surface with two independent winding directions. It can be embedded like a
        doughnut, but its intrinsic topology does not provide a shortcut through spacetime.</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \pi_1(T^2)=\mathbb Z\oplus\mathbb Z.
      \]</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>An inverted mapping torus \(\mathcal M_f\)</h3>
      <p>A global identification in which one circuit returns a torus fibre with reversed orientation. It
        can exist as a mathematical three-manifold without a mouth, throat or traveller.</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \mathcal M_f=\frac{T^2\times[0,1]}{(x,1)\sim(f(x),0)},
        \quad \det f_*=-1.
      \]</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>A Lorentzian wormhole</h3>
      <p>A spacetime connection between regions. Traversability requires a throat without an event horizon,
        finite tidal loads, a supportable stress tensor and a causal route that remains open long enough to
        use.</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        G_{\mu\nu}=8\pi T_{\mu\nu}
        \qquad (G=c=1).
      \]</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">The first historical distinction</p>
    <h2 id="t-einstein-rosen">An Einstein&ndash;Rosen Bridge Is Not a Traversable Passage</h2>
    <p>The maximally extended Schwarzschild solution contains a bridge between exterior regions. That
      bridge is hidden behind horizons and pinches off too quickly for a signal or traveller to pass from one
      exterior to the other. It supplies the ancestry of the modern word <em>wormhole</em>, not a usable
      tunnel. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>Rotating or charged black-hole solutions possess richer mathematical extensions, but their inner
      horizons are expected to be violently unstable. Drawing another asymptotic region on a causal diagram
      is not evidence that astrophysical collapse builds a safe door to it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">The traversability gate</p>
    <h2 id="t-morris-thorne">The Metric Must Say Where the Throat Is and What Supports It</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">For a static, spherically symmetric example, the Morris&ndash;Thorne form makes the
      debts visible. TTI does not get to erase them by changing the mouth&rsquo;s picture from a sphere to a
      torus.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame illuminated">
      <p>In geometric units \(G=c=1\), write</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        ds^2=-e^{2\Phi(r)}dt^2
        +\frac{dr^2}{1-b(r)/r}
        +r^2(d\theta^2+\sin^2\theta\,d\varphi^2).
      \]</p>
      <p>The throat lies at \(r=r_0\), where \(b(r_0)=r_0\). Traversability asks for finite \(\Phi\), so
        no horizon blocks the route, and for the flare-out condition \(b'(r_0)&lt;1\). At the throat,
        Einstein&rsquo;s equations then give a negative radial null contraction:</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        T_{\mu\nu}k^\mu k^\nu
        =\rho+p_r
        =\frac{b'(r_0)-1}{8\pi r_0^2}&lt;0.
      \]</p>
      <p>That violates the classical null energy condition for this simple traversable geometry.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> Quantum fields can produce restricted negative-energy effects, and
        specially constructed settings can make wormholes traversable in theoretical models. Neither fact
        demonstrates a macroscopic traversable wormhole in our universe.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

