<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Where inversion can enter</p>
    <h2 id="t-models">Three Ways to Relate a Wormhole to Inverted Tori</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid three">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Model A &mdash; global seam only</h3>
      <p>Spacetime has torus fibres with orientation-reversing monodromy. A long circuit changes a carried
        frame, but there are no local mouths and no shortcut. This is the least wormhole-like and the most
        faithful to the existing mapping-torus proposal.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Model B &mdash; one handle, two mouths</h3>
      <p>Two regions of one spacetime are connected by a throat. Each mouth has a toroidal state space or
        toroidal cross-section, and the gluing map reverses one winding direction:</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        (x,+)\sim(f(x),-),\qquad \det f_*=-1.
      \]</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Model C &mdash; bridge between instances</h3>
      <p>Separate toroidal universe-instances are joined by a higher-dimensional throat. This is the strongest
        claim: it requires a containing theory, a lawful boundary map and an account of conservation across
        worlds. No such theory is supplied here.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame">
      <p class="lede">TTI adopts none of these as established. Model A is already mathematically defined.
        Model B is the focused research question. Model C remains a metaphysical possibility until a larger
        dynamical framework gives &ldquo;between universes&rdquo; a physical meaning.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The proposed transport law</p>
    <h2 id="t-transport">A Mouth Map Can Reverse Orientation Without Reversing Time</h2>
    <p>Let a traveller or field state carry toroidal winding data \((p,q)\), an orientation sign \(\chi\),
      and ordinary future-directed four-momentum \(P^\mu\). A TTI mouth identification might act as</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      (p,q,\chi;P^\mu)_A
      \longmapsto
      \bigl(f_*(p,q),-\chi;\Lambda^\mu{}_{\nu}P^\nu\bigr)_B,
      \qquad \det f_*=-1.
    \]</p>
    <p>The spatial or internal frame reverses. The condition \(P^0&gt;0\) can still be preserved, so the
      mapping does not by itself reverse causal time. Nor does \(\chi\mapsto-\chi\) turn matter into
      antimatter, change electric charge or generate baryon number. Those effects would require additional
      field transformations and a conservation law. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>A complete model must also state whether \(f\) acts on physical space, an internal gauge space,
      polarization, a toroidal field configuration or only a coordinate chart. If no invariant measurement
      changes, the inversion is a relabelling rather than new physics.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Junction bookkeeping</p>
    <h2 id="t-junction">The Mouth Cannot Hide Its Stress&ndash;Energy</h2>
    <p>A thin-shell construction joins two geometries across a hypersurface with induced metric \(h_{ab}\).
      In geometric units, the jump in extrinsic curvature obeys the Israel junction condition</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      [K_{ab}]-h_{ab}[K]=-8\pi S_{ab}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The surface stress tensor \(S_{ab}\) is the price of the join. Choosing an orientation-reversing map
      does not set that price to zero. The bulk and shell must together respect local conservation, and any
      claimed transfer must balance flux through the two mouths:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \nabla_\mu T^{\mu\nu}=0,
      \qquad
      \int_A T^{\mu\nu}n_\mu\,d\Sigma
      +\int_B T^{\mu\nu}n_\mu\,d\Sigma
      +\Delta P^\nu_{\rm throat}=0.
    \]</p>
    <p class="muted">This schematic flux ledger does not derive the throat. It states what the proposal must
      conserve before words such as matter, memory or residue are allowed to cross it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">One passage and the return passage</p>
    <h2 id="t-two-crossings">Two Inversions Restore the Frame; They Do Not Automatically Erase the Journey</h2>
    <p>If the two mouth crossings apply the same ideal involution \(I\), then</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      I^2=\mathrm{id},
      \qquad \chi\xrightarrow{I}-\chi\xrightarrow{I}+\chi.
    \]</p>
    <p>The final orientation matches the initial orientation. Any remaining difference must live somewhere
      else: proper time along the path, a geometric phase, toroidal winding, interaction with the throat,
      environmental entanglement or material hysteresis. This is the same discipline used in
      <a href="/double-inversion/">Two Inversions</a>: restored state and remembered path are different
      questions.</p>
    <p>A wormhole therefore does not rescue residue by vocabulary. The proposal must name a state variable
      \(R\) and an evolution law. One possible bookkeeping form is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      R_{\rm out}=U_{\gamma}R_{\rm in},
      \qquad
      U_{\bar\gamma}U_{\gamma}=e^{i\Omega}
      \quad\text{or}
      \quad U_{\bar\gamma}U_{\gamma}=I.
    \]</p>
    <p>A non-zero phase \(\Omega\) or a non-trivial holonomy must be calculated from the connection. It cannot
      be inferred merely because the route was drawn through a throat. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame caution">
    <p class="kicker">Causality is not optional</p>
    <h2 id="t-causality">Moving the Mouths Can Turn a Shortcut into a Time Machine</h2>
    <p>If two traversable mouths acquire a relative time shift through acceleration or gravitational time
      dilation, the throat can participate in a closed timelike curve. That is a warning, not a feature.
      Chronology protection, quantum backreaction or instability may prevent the required configuration.</p>
    <p><strong>TTI therefore refuses this inference:</strong> temporal antipodes do not become synchronised
      merely because a diagram joins them. A path between opposite asymptotic limits still needs a global
      causal structure, and that structure must not permit contradiction or undefined initial data.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">What the sky and laboratory would need to distinguish</p>
    <h2 id="t-observables">A Wormhole Claim Must Outrun Black Holes, Lenses and Ordinary Topology</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Paired, parity-related images</h3>
      <p>A two-mouth model might produce correlated structures with a prescribed orientation reversal and
        time delay. Ordinary gravitational lensing also creates multiple images and parity flips, so the
        wormhole model must predict a correlation lensing cannot mimic.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Polarization holonomy</h3>
      <p>Transport around the complete route could rotate or reverse a polarization frame. Plasma, magnetic
        fields and ordinary gravitational Faraday rotation are foregrounds, not optional afterthoughts.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Ringdown and echoes</h3>
      <p>A horizonless compact throat may alter late-time wave propagation. Echo-like features are model
        dependent and contested; a claim needs a waveform family, priors and false-positive analysis.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Repeated cosmic structure</h3>
      <p>Global compact topology can correlate distant patterns. A local wormhole and a global mapping torus
        predict different repetition rules, allowing observations to separate Model A from Model B.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Energy and stability signatures</h3>
      <p>A supporting field may introduce lensing anomalies, fifth forces, vacuum instabilities or rapid
        collapse. A throat that survives only by hiding every supporting degree of freedom is not predictive.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>The clean loss</h3>
      <p>Reject the physical wormhole reading if no self-consistent metric and source exist, if perturbations
        destroy the throat, if chronology becomes inconsistent, or if its proposed signal reduces to an
        ordinary black hole, lens or coordinate relabelling.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Topological censorship</p>
    <h2 id="t-censorship">Why a Distant Observer May Never Be Allowed to Probe the Handle</h2>
    <p>Topological-censorship results show, under specified assumptions such as suitable causal structure,
      asymptotic behaviour and averaged energy conditions, that causal observers cannot use non-trivial
      topology as an observable shortcut. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> The theorem is not the sentence
      &ldquo;wormholes never exist&rdquo;; it is a list of conditions a traversable proposal must violate or evade.</p>
    <p>That makes the TTI task sharper. It must declare which assumption changes, why the change is physically
      allowed and what other consequence follows. &ldquo;The torus is inverted&rdquo; is not an exemption.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">A six-gate research programme</p>
    <h2 id="t-programme">What Must Be Built Before the Word &ldquo;Wormhole&rdquo; Is Earned</h2>
  </div>
  <ol class="timeline">
    <li><strong>Specify the manifold and metric.</strong> State whether the mouths are spherical, toroidal or
      toroidal only in an internal state space; give the global identifications and causal boundaries.</li>
    <li><strong>Solve for the source.</strong> Derive \(T_{\mu\nu}\) or a modified-gravity equivalent, test
      energy conditions and account for every shell or boundary term.</li>
    <li><strong>Prove traversability.</strong> Exclude horizons and singularities along the route, bound tidal
      acceleration and give a finite crossing time.</li>
    <li><strong>Test stability.</strong> Perturb the throat, supporting fields and mouth map. A static drawing
      that collapses under arbitrarily small disturbances is not a transport mechanism.</li>
    <li><strong>Protect causality and conservation.</strong> Evolve both mouths consistently, prevent or explain
      closed timelike curves, and conserve energy, momentum and every claimed charge.</li>
    <li><strong>Predeclare a discriminator.</strong> Predict a parity, timing, polarization, lensing or wave
      signature that an ordinary compact object and an ordinary lens do not already provide.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h2 id="t-ledger">Earned, Chosen, Refused</h2>
    <dl class="glossary">
      <dt>Earned</dt>
      <dd>General relativity admits bridge and wormhole geometries. The elementary static traversable throat
        requires null-energy-condition violation at its flare-out. Mapping tori and orientation-reversing
        automorphisms are precise mathematical objects. A global identification, a local throat and a
        coordinate picture are distinct.</dd>
      <dt>Chosen</dt>
      <dd>That a physical mouth carries toroidal winding data; that its gluing map has determinant \(-1\);
        that the TTI seam can be localised as a wormhole; and that a measurable phase or residue survives a
        round trip.</dd>
      <dt>Refused</dt>
      <dd>Calling the hole in a doughnut a wormhole. Treating an Einstein&ndash;Rosen bridge as traversable.
        Claiming that inversion supplies negative energy, stability or chronology protection. Turning
        orientation reversal into time reversal, antimatter or memory without an explicit field law.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

